it was supposed to be a speech for new  graduates of his paramilitary rapid  support forces but on tuesday the deputy  head of sudan's sovereignty council once  again turned his attention to the  civilians in the transitional government  a week after an attempted coup tension  between the military and civilian sides  of the administration remains at an  all-time high  they blame us we are part of the  partnership politics are based on things  i don't like but i've learned my lessons  i'm a military man the council was  created to have all the decisions  and after that they said it was a  council for consultation only but if  you're a council for consultation you  can't oblige people to apply and  implement your decisions  sudan's transitional government came to  power months after former president  ahmad bashir was overthrown in april  2019 the power sharing administration  was intended to lead the country into a  democratic system by early 2024  but tensions between the ruling  political coalition that forms the  civilian side and the military  have raised concerns about the future of  the partnership for months political  parties have demanded reforms in the  military they say like other  institutions the army contains members  loyal to bashir's regime and needs to be  restructured  that angered the military and the coup  attempt has further fueled calls for its  reform  we are committed to evaluating this  transitional period we are capable of  amending it but it's only us who will  amend it because we are the ones  representing you no other person can  change this equation  a war of words between the two sides has  been followed by the army with drawing  protection from the committee formed to  dismantle the former ruling party and  fight corruption  that led to protests in the capital  khartoum in show of support for the  civilian leadership the sudanese  professionals association which  spare-headed anti-government protests  that led to the overthrow of president  bashir called for an end to the power  sharing agreement the un has urged both  sides to preserve the partnership  [Music]  this partnership model is a unique  approach which can lead sudan to a  complete democratic transition of  civilian rule and peace  i urge all sides to tone down on the  media rhetoric and to focus on dialogue  and cooperation  political differences are natural but  there must be cooperation for the sake  of the country  while tension between the two sides  remains those who let protests against  the former government have called for  more demonstrations against the military  they say that despite any shortcomings  among political parties they still want  to ensure that civilians have the upper  hand in running the country and not the  military hebrew morgan al-jazeera  hartung
 

